        text: 'An extended metaphor takes a comparison and sustains it across multiple lines or even an entire poem, developing its logic and discovering where the comparison illuminates and where it strains. Emily Dickinson\'s poem beginning "Hope is the thing with feathers" extends the comparison of hope to a bird throughout the entire poem: the bird perches in the soul, sings without words, keeps going through the worst storms, and never asks for anything in return. Each new detail of the bird deepens the understanding of hope — its persistence, its voicelessness, its resilience. The poet does not need to name hope directly after the first line. The metaphor does the work. Extended metaphors are powerful because they require the reader to hold two things in mind simultaneously — the vehicle (the bird) and the tenor (hope) — and the friction between them generates the poem\'s meaning.',
