        text: 'A camera does not simply record — it argues. Every choice of shot type is a choice of meaning. An extreme long shot places the subject within a vast environment, making them appear small, exposed, or lost: the lone figure crossing a desert is not merely walking; they are dwarfed by the world around them. A close-up does the opposite: it fills the frame with a face, a hand, or a single significant object, demanding that the viewer pay attention, that they read the emotion in the crease of an eye or the tremble of a lip. The medium shot — chest to head — is the language of conversation; it gives us enough of the body to read gesture and emotion without making interpretation an act of forced intimacy. Shot type is grammar. The director writes sentences in frames. Just as a novelist chooses words, the director chooses distance; and distance — emotional as well as physical — is the subject of every scene.',
