        text: 'Lighting in cinema is not illumination — it is information. High-key lighting, characterised by bright, even light with few shadows, fills a scene with an openness associated with safety, comedy, and romance: the well-lit kitchen of a family sitcom, the gleaming operating theatre of a medical drama, the bright outdoor spaces of a romantic comedy. Low-key lighting creates deep shadows, stark contrasts between light and dark, and pockets of obscurity in which danger or deception can hide. The noir detective film perfected this visual language: the shadowed face of an informant, the single lamp casting a cone of light on an empty chair, the street-corner figure half-illuminated by a flickering sign. Colour temperature adds a further layer: cool blue tones suggest isolation, clinical detachment, or threat; warm amber and gold suggest safety, memory, and belonging. Directors use light not to show us what is in a scene, but to tell us how to feel about it.',
