        label: 'Passage A — Mise-en-scène: Everything in the Frame',
        text: 'Mise-en-scène — a French term meaning "putting into the scene" — describes everything that appears within the camera\'s frame: the setting, the costumes, the props, the position and expression of the actors, the lighting, and the colour palette. It is the visual world the director constructs for the camera to record. Every element within the frame is a decision — and decisions communicate. A cluttered, overcrowded room suggests disorder, anxiety, or a character who cannot let go of the past. A spare, minimal space suggests control, ambition, or emotional coldness. Costume is biography: the colour, condition, and style of what a character wears tells us who they are before they speak. A pristine white dress in a darkening room is not merely clothing — it is irony, vulnerability, or dangerous innocence depending on the narrative. Props carry symbolic weight: the ticking clock on the mantelpiece, the unanswered letter on the desk. Nothing in a well-made film is there by accident.',
