        text: 'The Globe Theatre, built on the south bank of the Thames in 1599, was not merely an entertainment venue — it was a civic institution at the centre of London\'s public life. It could hold up to three thousand people, accommodating every social stratum: groundlings who paid a penny to stand in the open yard before the stage, and wealthier patrons who paid more to sit in the covered galleries above. Unlike the court masques performed for the exclusive pleasure of the monarch and the aristocracy, the public theatre was genuinely democratic in its reach. It brought the same language, the same stories, and the same moral questions before an audience of merchants, apprentices, craftsmen, servants, and nobles simultaneously. This democratising function was not incidental — it was subversive. A theatre that placed a king on stage and showed him making catastrophic mistakes was implicitly arguing that power was not divine but human, and human things could be questioned.',
